@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+Client-side performance monitoring
+==================================
+
+The homepage loads `performance-monitor.js` without dependencies. It measures
+clicks on navigation links, Order Now, and anywhere inside a Seasonal Favorites
+card (including its View Details link). Delegated capture listeners also cover
+keyboard-generated clicks and elements inserted later, without blocking links.
+
+Read the latest 100 samples with `window.dailyGrindPerformance.getSamples()`.
+Connect analytics by listening for `daily-grind:performance` on `window`; each
+event's `detail` contains `id`, `kind`, `label`, `startTime`, `duration` in
+milliseconds, and `method`. No data is sent externally. A User Timing measure
+named `daily-grind:<kind>:<label>` is also emitted for PerformanceObservers and
+then cleared to avoid an unbounded performance timeline.
+
+For clicks, `event-timing` uses the browser's Event Timing duration from input to
+the next paint, including input delay. That API rounds durations and omits fast
+events. Unsupported browsers and omitted events use `animation-frame-estimate`:
+two animation frames allow a rendering opportunity after event handlers finish.
+This is an estimate of the next visual update, not the completion of animations,
+image loading, or arbitrary asynchronous work. See the
+[Event Timing specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/event-timing/).
+
+Synchronous client-side view switches through `pushState`, `replaceState`,
+`popstate`, and `hashchange` are timed automatically using the frame estimate.
+These are history-operation-to-paint estimates, not network navigation timings.
+History state updates that do not render a view still produce a sample; a back
+navigation that changes a hash can produce both popstate and hashchange samples.
+The existing homepage uses normal document links and has no client-side router;
+monitoring does not turn those links into client-side routes. A full-page unload
+may prevent a click sample from completing; destination paints are not measured.
+
+For an asynchronous view switch, mark both the beginning and the DOM commit:
+
+```js
+const committed = window.dailyGrindPerformance.beginViewSwitch('menu');
+const menu = await loadMenu();
+renderMenu(menu);
+committed(); // For frameworks, call from the post-commit hook/effect instead.
+```
+
+This explicit sample includes loading/rendering time plus a rendering opportunity
+after commit. Calling its completion function twice has no effect. It can coexist
+with the shorter automatic history sample; use labels to distinguish them. Use
+fixed labels rather than URLs or user data. Samples pending when the document is
+hidden or leaves the page are discarded, since paint timings would be misleading.
